<p>&nbsp;The clinic provides breast lump assessment for patients experiencing a new lump, breast pain, nipple discharge, skin changes or an abnormal imaging finding. The service involves focused clinical evaluation, with an estimated consultation fee of 600 EGP. Benign breast conditions are also assessed and treated when appropriate, including fibroadenomas, cysts and other non-cancerous conditions. Procedures for benign conditions start from an estimated 16,500 EGP.</p>
<p>&nbsp;Breast cancer surgery is offered through individualised surgical planning, including breast-conserving surgery and mastectomy when clinically indicated. The estimated starting fee is 50,000 EGP. Oncoplastic and reconstructive surgery combines consideration of cancer safety, breast shape and the reconstructive options available to the patient, with estimated fees starting from 75,000 EGP. Aesthetic breast surgery consultations are available for breast reduction, breast lift and reshaping, with treatment plans adapted to each patient's anatomy and goals. The consultation fee is estimated at 600 EGP. Post-operative follow-up includes wound review, recovery guidance and continued care after breast procedures. The estimated fee is 500 EGP and is included for 30 days after clinic surgery. All prices are estimated starting fees, and the final written quotation is confirmed following examination and development of an individual treatment plan.</p>
<p>&nbsp;Patients are encouraged to seek assessment when they notice a change in the breast. A new lump, persistent pain, nipple discharge, nipple inversion or a change in the skin should receive appropriate medical assessment. Although most breast symptoms are not cancer, they should not be ignored. The clinic's approach to consultation is designed to provide clarity at every stage, so that patients understand what is known, what will happen next and why. The consultation begins by listening to the patient's concerns, symptoms and priorities rather than relying only on a scan or report. This is followed by careful examination and, when required, appropriate imaging or investigations. The findings and available options are then explained clearly, and if treatment is necessary, the plan includes preparation, recovery and follow-up.</p>
<p>&nbsp;Dr Mostafa Sedky is a consultant surgeon specialising in breast surgery, with experience in breast assessment, breast cancer surgery and reconstructive care. He is a Member of the Royal College of Surgeons of Edinburgh, a physician registered with the UK General Medical Council and an Assistant Lecturer of Surgery at New Giza University. He also completed a fellowship at Baheya Breast Cancer Hospital, giving him dedicated experience in breast cancer care.</p>
<p>&nbsp;The patient gallery presents consented cases intended to help explain possible surgical outcomes. The examples include treatment of male gynaecomastia, described as scarless liposuction-based surgery performed under local anaesthetic; breast augmentation using autologous fat transferred from the banana roll area; scarless breast Laser-Endolift performed under local anaesthesia; and breast augmentation using a 250 CC Mentor high-profile, non-textured implant placed in a dual-plane position. The clinic emphasises that individual patients and surgical results vary, and photographs do not guarantee an identical outcome.</p>
<p>&nbsp;The clinic also provides a video library containing short explanations of common questions concerning breast health, investigations, surgery and recovery, accompanied by written summaries. Topics include the limitations of hormone-receptor pathology in selected breast cancers and the possible role of genomic testing, reasons chemotherapy may be given before breast cancer surgery, situations in which breast MRI can provide additional information, nutrition and hydration after breast surgery, reconstructive approaches using implants, tissue-based techniques and oncoplastic surgery, and arm swelling following axillary lymph-node surgery. Other topics address retracted nipples, skin- and nipple-sparing mastectomy, mammogram radiation, situations in which chemotherapy may not be required for breast cancer, healthy eating and cancer risk, radiotherapy in patients with breast implants, breast implants and rare implant-capsule conditions, gynaecomastia, and the importance of listening, informed consent, realistic expectations and reliable follow-up in breast surgery care.</p>
<p>&nbsp;Patient feedback published by the clinic shows a displayed rating of 5.0 based on 12 published reviews. Patients describe Dr Sedky positively in relation to his medical knowledge, character, communication and surgical results. Reviews mention experiences involving breast augmentation, breast reduction and surgery for benign breast tumours, with patients describing satisfaction with their treatment and aesthetic outcomes.</p>
<p>&nbsp;The clinic also offers a promotional care-gift feature in which visitors can enter their contact details and spin for one of five possible clinic gifts. The listed possibilities include a 10% discount on surgeries, a free consultation, a 25% discount on clinic procedures, free post-operative care consultation and a 50% discount on consultations. The clinic states that contact details are stored securely for issuing the prize and contacting the participant about redemption, and advises visitors not to enter medical information.</p>
<p>&nbsp;The medical blog provides evidence-informed information about breast symptoms, investigations, surgery and recovery. Recent articles include guidance on when a breast lump should be checked, explaining that most breast lumps are benign but that every new or persistent change deserves proper clinical assessment. Another article discusses corsets and support bras after breast surgery, explaining their possible role in comfort and limiting breast movement while noting that they do not reliably prevent bleeding or seroma and should never be excessively tight. An article on intraoperative ultrasound explains how real-time imaging can help localise and remove a breast lesion visible on ultrasound while assisting with preservation of healthy tissue, while emphasising that pathology remains the final test.</p>
<p>&nbsp;The clinic is located in Abbasiyah, Cairo. Patients are advised to use the clinic pin in Google Maps or contact the clinic team for the exact entrance and directions. The website also provides common questions for patients preparing for a first visit, including what to bring to the consultation, whether every breast lump requires surgery, whether reconstruction can be discussed during the same appointment and whether a family member can attend.</p>
<p>&nbsp;The clinic can be contacted by telephone at +201100781565 or through WhatsApp. The website describes its service as consultant-led breast care in Cairo, covering breast assessment, cancer surgery, oncoplastic surgery, reconstructive surgery and aesthetic breast procedures. It also states that the information provided is educational and does not replace an in-person medical assessment.</p>
<p>&nbsp;Dr Mostafa Khaled Mohamed Gamaleldin Sedky is identified as a Consultant Breast Surgeon and MRCS Edinburgh. His stated clinical interests include breast assessment, breast cancer surgery, oncoplastic and reconstructive surgery and aesthetic breast procedures. His listed credentials include membership of the Royal College of Surgeons of Edinburgh, registration with the UK General Medical Council, completion of a fellowship at Baheya Breast Cancer Hospital and an academic appointment as Assistant Lecturer of Surgery at New Giza University.</p>
<p>&nbsp;The professional profile lists his MRCS Edinburgh membership and GMC registration number 7788172. It also identifies his Baheya Breast Cancer Hospital fellowship and his Assistant Lecturer position at New Giza University. The profile directs readers toward official or publicly available records where possible, including the Royal College of Surgeons of Edinburgh member-search service, the General Medical Council register, Baheya's official website and the New Giza University website.</p>
<p>&nbsp;His listed academic and research work includes a 2024 journal article in QJM concerning evaluation of targeted axillary dissection in node-positive locally advanced breast cancer patients who achieved complete pathological response after neoadjuvant chemotherapy. Other listed publications from 2022 concern paediatric obesity and anaesthetic challenges of metabolic surgery, and the effects of dasatinib on autophagic flux, apoptosis and macrophage infiltration in the kidneys of obese mice. A 2026 oral abstract presentation concerns the clinical and educational impact of an Egyptian advanced trauma training course using silicon-based trauma simulators. The profile also provides researcher identifiers and links to ORCID, Google Scholar, ResearchGate and PubMed so that the professional information can be compared with indexed research records.</p>
